<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kiwalaʻō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kiwalao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kiwalaʻō was the son and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a>, ruler of the island of Hawaiʻi. On his father's death in 1782 he inherited the island, while his cousin Kamehameha received custody of the war god Kūkaʻilimoku. Disputes over the distribution of land led to war, and Kiwalaʻō was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mokuohai">Battle of Mokuʻōhai</a> in July 1782, opening the way for Kamehameha's rise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiwalao.jpg" alt="Kiwalaʻō" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bishop Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cloak_of_Kiwalao_(1899).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Hawaiʻi whom his uncle, the ruling chief Kalaniʻōpuʻu, had made guardian of the war god Kūkāʻilimoku, fell out with Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s heir after the old ruler’s death in 1782 and defeated him, his cousin <b>Kīwalaʻō</b>, at the Battle of Mokuʻōhai, winning the west and north of the island.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1782 Kamehameha, guardian of the war god Kūkāʻilimoku, defeated his cousin <b>Kīwalaʻō</b>, Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s son and successor, at Mokuʻōhai, and by 1795, with Western guns, ships and advisers, he had conquered every main island but Kauaʻi and Niʻihau, whose ruler accepted him as overlord in 1810.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>After Kalaniʻōpuʻu died in 1782, his nephew Kamehameha defeated <b>Kiwalaʻō</b> at Mokuʻōhai and, after years of war, won the whole island by 1791.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mokuohai">Battle of Mokuohai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.46,-155.92&amp;year=1772" aria-label="Battle of Mokuohai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="337 1443 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kiwalaʻō"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M787 1713h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M787 1713h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=19.46,-155.92&amp;year=1772">What was here in 1772? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
